BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:OrionidMeteorsPeak
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20101021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20101021
DTSTAMP:20260224T194005Z
UID:9e72edc6-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:eg425gt6gqfpr48e9osmn0l3tg@google.com\nOctober 21\, 22 - Orioni
 ds Meteor Shower. The Orionids is an average shower producing about 20 met
 eors per hour at their peak. This shower usually peaks on the 21st\, but i
 t is highly irregular. A good show could be experienced on any morning fro
 m October 20 - 24\, and some meteors may be seen any time from October 17 
 - 25. Best viewing will be to the east after midnight.fromseaandsky.com\n1
 0/21 Orionids meteor shower (major activity- normally 10 to 20 per hour) p
 eaks at 12:00 p.m. \n      Duration is from 15th to 29th.  Observing and h
 istory: \n      http://meteorshowersonline.com/orionids.html \n\nKD12/29/2
 009
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mercury at Greatest Eastern Elongation
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20190227
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20190227
DTSTAMP:20260224T194005Z
UID:9e7689a4-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertf6f37ac3c8f90d825403bf9c610aa4da\nFebruary 27 - Mercu
 ry at Greatest Eastern Elongation. The planet Mercury reaches greatest eas
 tern elongation of 18.1 degrees from the Sun. This is the best time to vie
 w Mercury since it will be at its highest point above the horizon in the e
 vening sky. Look for the planet low in the western sky just after sunset.\
 n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-201
 9.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
