DESCRIPTION:odosc70p3jm7ctam28d5rp0j8c@google.com\nTonight's Class is Cance
 lled from Tom\n\nAAAP Members\, \n\nI have scheduled the Better Observing 
 Techniques Classes for Sep 2 & 3 and Sep 30 & Oct 1 (Wednesdays & Thursday
 s) at Wagman Observatory starting at 7:30 PM\, weather permitting.  If you
  need assistance with your scopes\, collimation of your Newtonian scopes o
 r other optics\, bring them to these sessions.\n \nTom Reiland
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:March Equinox
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170320
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170320
DTSTAMP:20260224T194005Z
UID:9eb5526a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertc9f10efcba673313eb008bb4d342fde4\nMarch 20 - March Eq
 uinox. The March equinox occurs at 10:29 UTC. The Sun will shine directly 
 on the equator and there will be nearly equal amounts of day and night thr
 oughout the world. This is also the first day of spring (vernal equinox) i
 n the Northern Hemisphere and the first day of fall (autumnal equinox) in 
 the Southern Hemisphere.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astrono
 my/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy
 -calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20181222
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20181222
DTSTAMP:20260224T194005Z
UID:9eb8c4c2-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert61d955669fcf749d461a3849b197fe19\nDecember 22 - Full 
 Moon. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Su
 n and its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 17:
 49 UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Fu
 ll Cold Moon because this is the time of year when the cold winter air set
 tles in and the nights become long and dark. This moon has also been known
  as the Full Long Nights Moon and the Moon Before Yule.\n\nSource: <a href
 ="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www
 .seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
