BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kunkle Star Party
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20130803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20130803
DTSTAMP:20260224T194006Z
UID:9f10dec8-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:n1m99788nlto1qlhj93p4b5r0o@google.com\nNo start time provided.\
 n\nThe following dates have been scheduled for star parties at Kunkle Park
 \, Washington Twp.\, Westmoreland County.  It’s about 5 or 6 minutes sou
 th of Apollo\, PA\, and about ½ mile off Rte. 66.  I’d guess it’s a h
 alf hour ride east of Wagman Observatory.  I’ll get directions to those 
 who are interested.\n\n \n\nFRIDAY\, May 17\, 1st quarter moon  \n\n \n\nS
 ATURDAY\, May 25\, full moon \n\n(Two May events because the weather is so
  unpredictable.  Let’s actually try to get one in for May and we never s
 how the full moon\, even though it will strongly limit what else we can se
 e that night)\n\n \n\nSATURDAY\, August 3\, near new moon (dark sky event\
 , and this is a reasonably dark site\, even with lights on the police stat
 ion)\n\n \n\nSATURDAY\, October 12\, 1st quarter moon \n\n(Possibly Washin
 gton Twp. Halloween Party night for the kids)\n\n \n\nWe could use your he
 lp.  We’ve been advertising the events more the past year or so and our 
 attendance is slowly growing.  Last summer we got more than 50 people on a
  crummy night.\n\n \n\nPlease let me know if and what dates you can help.\
 n\n \n\nThanks a million.\n\n \n\nTerry\n\n \n\n\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Orionids
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20141022
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20141022
DTSTAMP:20260224T194006Z
UID:9f14a8be-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:qgq9vfv42avoai85cjfbtcq7ks@google.com\nOctober 22\, 23 - Orioni
 ds Meteor Shower. The Orionids is an average shower producing up to 20 met
 eors per hour at its peak. It is produced by dust grains left behind by co
 met Halley\, which has been known and observed since ancient times. The sh
 ower runs annually from October 2 to November 7. It peaks this year on the
  night of October 21 and the morning of October 22. This will be an excell
 ent year for the Orionids because there will be no moon to interfere with 
 the show. Best viewing will be from a dark location after midnight. Meteor
 s will radiate from the constellation Orion\, but can appear anywhere in t
 he sky.\nhttp://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2014.html\n\nK
 D 12/22/2013
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
