BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Aurigid Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20070901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20070901
DTSTAMP:20260224T194006Z
UID:9f2d725e-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:20080312T190939Z-4992161-32526@www.3ap.org\nTHE 2007 AURIGIDS\n
 \nThe Earth will travel through the dust trail of long-period comet C/1911
  N1\n(Kiess)\, September 1\, 2007\, about 11:36 +/- 20 minutes UT (= 07:36
  a.m.\nEastern Daylight Savings Time). This trail is not crossed again in 
 the next\n70 years. This the only known long-period comet with such dust\n
 trail crossings.  A 2-hour outburst of bright (-3 to +3 magnitude) meteors
 \nradiating from the constellation Auriga (Radiant at RA = 92\, Decl. = +3
 9\,\nspeed Vinf = 67 km/s)\, has been predicted to produce perhaps 400 - 1
 000\nmeteors per hour. The meteors will be\nbright enough for the display 
 not to be dimmed much by\, the 4 days past Full Moon high in the sky\, but
  sunrise\, may present a problem for us! Best viewing is towards\nthe East
  and NorthEast. Best to start watching at least an hour before the\npredic
 ted peak time\, then see the rate of meteors increase and decrease\nwhile 
 Earth travels through the shower. The peak of the shower is predicted\nto 
 occur at 11:37 GMT.  Unfortunately this comes during daylight for Europe\n
 and much of North America.  But the western United States and Canada\, as\
 nwell as much of Alaska and Hawaii will still be in pre-dawn darkness and\
 nwould be in an excellent position to view it.\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
