BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MembersPicnic
DTSTART:20140920T200000Z
DTEND:20140920T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f8224d4-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:bajqoiuhiea8v5j3c624462ha8@google.com\nThere will be a members
 ’-only picnic\, on September 20th at the Mingo Creek Park Observatory. T
 he picnic will begin at 4:00 pm with the observatory being opened for sola
 r and dark sky observing. Refreshments and hot dogs will be provided by th
 e AAAP.\nEveryone is asked to bring a salad or desert or their favorite di
 sh to share with the other members. More specific information will follow 
 on the AAAP Yahoo Group and in the September Guide Star. Note: September 2
 0th is also the weekend of the Mingo Park Covered Bridge Festival. Some me
 mbers may consider coming down early and taking in the festival before the
  picnic. One of the festival venues is at the ball field area in the park\
 , which is about a 15 minute walk from the observatory.
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jupiter at Opposition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170407
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170407
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f881ff6-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert7a34bc3be1947121a62e681b6d91e5f2\nApril 7 - Jupiter a
 t Opposition. The giant planet will be at its closest approach to Earth an
 d its face will be fully illuminated by the Sun. It will be brighter than 
 any other time of the year and will be visible all night long. This is the
  best time to view and photograph Jupiter and its moons. A medium-sized te
 lescope should be able to show you some of the details in Jupiter's cloud 
 bands. A good pair of binoculars should allow you to see Jupiter's four la
 rgest moons appearing as bright dots on either side of the planet.\n\nSour
 ce: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html"
 >http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wagman Observatory Star Party
DTSTART:20220604T004500Z
DTEND:20220604T014500Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f8b0bc6-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert87b3bfad834801d6305e33bcbdc76ec3\nPLEASE NOTE: Star p
 arty activities may be limited or canceled due to weather. Dark sky viewin
 g begins after sunset. Event start times prior to sunset may include prese
 ntations or lectures.\n\nAdditional Info:  https://3ap.org/resources/star-
 parties/\n\nFor Moon\, Sun\, and Planetary Rise and Set Times\, please vis
 it the Time and Date website at:\n<a href="https://www.timeanddate.com/ast
 ronomy">https://www.timeanddate.com/astronomy</a>
LOCATION:Wagman Observatory
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170906
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f8e0e20-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert9d182e581a45d5684e70b856c702b5e2\nSeptember 6 - Full 
 Moon. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Su
 n and its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 07:
 03 UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Fu
 ll Corn Moon because the corn is harvested around this time of year.\n\nSo
 urce: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.htm
 l">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Draconids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171007
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171007
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f93937c-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertbf9c1d44a480c95a738ea5b054bc0e16\nOctober 7 - Draconi
 ds Meteor Shower. The Draconids is a minor meteor shower producing only ab
 out 10 meteors per hour. It is produced by dust grains left behind by come
 t 21P Giacobini-Zinner which was first discovered in 1900. The Draconids i
 s an unusual shower in that the best viewing is in the early evening inste
 ad of early morning like most other showers. The shower runs annually from
  October 6-10 and peaks this year on the the night of the 7th. Unfortunate
 ly the nearly full moon will block all but the brightest meteors this year
 . If you are extremely patient you may be able to catch a few good ones. B
 est viewing will be in the early evening from a dark location far away fro
 m city lights. Meteors will radiate from the constellation Draco but can a
 ppear anywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astro
 nomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astrono
 my-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mercury at Greatest Western Elongation
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20181215
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20181215
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f968a32-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert55312e27951a768fa709fc39bebd6b39\nDecember 15 - Mercu
 ry at Greatest Western Elongation. The planet Mercury reaches greatest wes
 tern elongation of 21.3 degrees from the Sun. This is the best time to vie
 w Mercury since it will be at its highest point above the horizon in the m
 orning sky. Look for the planet low in the eastern sky just before sunrise
 .\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2
 018.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171218
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171218
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f99a1a4-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert9189d8a445494c83e1e43ac17987b325\nDecember 18 - New M
 oon. The Moon will located on the same side of the Earth as the Sun and wi
 ll not be visible in the night sky. This phase occurs at 06:30 UTC. This i
 s the best time of the month to observe faint objects such as galaxies and
  star clusters because there is no moonlight to interfere.\n\nSource: <a h
 ref="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://
 www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Taurids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20181105
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20181105
DTSTAMP:20260224T194007Z
UID:9f9cf002-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConverte685ca2c6420d5ee14134b8ca6e86327\nNovember 5-6 - Taur
 ids Meteor Shower. The Taurids is a long-running minor meteor shower produ
 cing only about 5-10 meteors per hour. It is unusual in that it consists o
 f two separate streams. The first is produced by dust grains left behind b
 y Asteroid 2004 TG10. The second stream is produced by debris left behind 
 by Comet 2P Encke. The shower runs annually from September 7 to December 1
 0. It peaks this year on the the night of November 5. The thin crescent mo
 on will set early in the evening leaving dark skies for viewing. Best view
 ing will be just after midnight from a dark location far away from city li
 ghts. Meteors will radiate from the constellation Taurus but can appear an
 ywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/ast
 ronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calen
 dar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
