BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Starhopping Class
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20140915T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20140915T200000
DTSTAMP:20260224T194008Z
UID:a016240e-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:aa7or6r0jrjntk37v2c278okl0@google.com\nAn announcement from Tom
  Reiland\, Director of Wagman Observatory:\nI'll be conducting classes on 
 how to find celestial objects without the use of a stinking computer. The 
 method is called Starhopping and it's how I navigate around the night sky 
 with my nude eyes\, binoculars and telescopes. If I had been using a Goto 
 system all these year\, I would have not discovered Reiland's Cluster (or 
 Reiland 1) and the Supernova in the Whirlpool Galaxy.\nThese classes will 
 be on four nights. It will be the same session each night. You choose whic
 h one is convenient for you. They start at 7 PM and last until all of the 
 students have left. Classes will be on Mondays and Tuesdays. Sept 15 and 1
 6 and Oct 13 and 14 at Wagman Observatory.\nThey're free\, but you can mak
 e a donation if you want. Let me know if yinz are interested 'n'at. (Tom R
 eiland\, Instructor)
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Leonid meteor shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20161117
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20161117
DTSTAMP:20260224T194008Z
UID:a018db90-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:0df64oqjampkv7dp1peroogb8g@google.com\nThe Leonid meteor shower
  will reach its maximum rate of activity on 18 November 2016. Some shootin
 g stars associated with the shower are expected to be visible each night f
 rom 15 Nov to 20 Nov. The maximum rate of meteors expected to be visible f
 rom a dark location is around 20 per hour (ZHR). The Moon will be 19 days 
 old at the time of peak activity\, and so will present significant interfe
 rence in the pre-dawn sky.\n\nSource: <a href="https://in-the-sky.org/news
 .php?id=20161118_11_100">https://in-the-sky.org/news.php?id=20161118_11_10
 0</a>\n\nLook for the Leonids November 15-20\, 2016.  Unfortunately 2016's
  peak of pre-dawn Nov. 18 will suffer from moonlight.
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Conjunction of Venus and Jupiter
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20191124
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20191124
DTSTAMP:20260224T194008Z
UID:a01d9f40-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertaa0cb964fc6f043ed4e2297f24cf5eae\nNovember 24 - Conju
 nction of Venus and Jupiter. A conjunction of Venus and Jupiter will be vi
 sible on November 24. The two bright planets will be visible within 1.4 de
 grees of each other in the evening sky. Look for this impressive sight in 
 the western sky just after sunset.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.o
 rg/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy
 /astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Partial Lunar Eclipse
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170807
DTSTAMP:20260224T194008Z
UID:a0204178-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert3c4e9dfb15f5433d7c325de7016a9a9c\nAugust 7 - Partial 
 Lunar Eclipse. A partial lunar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes through
  the Earth's partial shadow (or penumbra) and only a portion of it passes 
 through the darkest shadow (or umbra). During this type of eclipse a part 
 of the Moon will darken as it moves through the Earth's shadow. The eclips
 e will be visible throughout most of eastern Africa\; central Asia\; the I
 ndian Ocean\; and Australia. \n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/as
 tronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astr
 onomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
