BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Delta Aquarids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180728
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180728
DTSTAMP:20260224T194009Z
UID:a0880704-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConverte4b03d64e960e2345ed9e374fbe9899f\nJuly 28-29 - Delta 
 Aquarids Meteor Shower. The Delta Aquarids is an average shower that can p
 roduce up to 20 meteors per hour at its peak. It is produced by debris lef
 t behind by comets Marsden and Kracht. The shower runs annually from July 
 12 to August 23. It peaks this year on the night of July 28 and morning of
  July 29. The nearly full moon will be a problem this year blocking out al
 l but the brightest meteors. But if you are patient you should still be ab
 le to catch a few good ones. Best viewing will be from a dark location aft
 er midnight. Meteors will radiate from the constellation Aquarius but can 
 appear anywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astr
 onomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astron
 omy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:GLOBEatNight
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20090316
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20090316
DTSTAMP:20260224T194009Z
UID:a08cd964-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:ate5ibbuv0to3lq1oj4ed2pkik@google.com\nPublic sky brightness su
 rvey.\nGo to "http://www.globe.gov/GaN/"\n\nThe five easy star-hunting ste
 ps\, for which more information is provided online\, are: 1. Find your lat
 itude and longitude. 2. Find Orion by going outside an hour after sunset (
 about 7-10pm local time) 3. Match your nighttime sky to magnitude charts. 
 4. Report your observation on GaN website. (Observations can be made March
  16 through March 28. 5. Compare your observation to thousands around the 
 world. \n\nKD\n\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
