BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:TotalLunarEclipse
DTSTART:20141008T102500Z
DTEND:20141008T112500Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a1271646-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:8cnfedrvqrkiq6qu9beftme0k4@google.com\nmorning of Oct 8 and is 
 best viewed in the Pacific Coast area\, though it will be visible here fro
 m 6:25 to 7:24 AM.  Sunrise is close to 7:24 AM that morning.  That's a We
 dnesday morning. We can open both observatories\, weather permitting\, to 
 any night owls or early morning risers who are interested in viewing them 
 through our scopes.  Maybe we can get some morning TV news teams to send a
  reporter and camera person to the observatories.\n \nas per Tom R 11/26/2
 013\n\nOctober 8 - Total Lunar Eclipse. A total lunar eclipse occurs when 
 the Moon passes completely through the Earth's dark shadow\, or umbra. Dur
 ing this type of eclipse\, the Moon will gradually get darker and then tak
 e on a rusty or blood red color. The eclipse will be visible throughout mo
 st of North America\, South America\, eastern Asia\, and Australia. (NASA 
 Map and Eclipse Information)  http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-ca
 lendar-2014.html\n\nKD 11/26/2013
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Orionids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20191021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20191021
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a12a1990-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert2952deacf42b0bac9f347b461155f2d5\nOctober 21-22 - Ori
 onids Meteor Shower. The Orionids is an average shower producing up to 20 
 meteors per hour at its peak. It is produced by dust grains left behind by
  comet Halley which has been known and observed since ancient times. The s
 hower runs annually from October 2 to November 7. It peaks this year on th
 e night of October 21 and the morning of October 22. The second quarter mo
 on will block some of the fainter meteors this year but the Orionids tend 
 to be fairly bright so it could still be a good show. Best viewing will be
  from a dark location after midnight. Meteors will radiate from the conste
 llation Orion but can appear anywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a href="http
 ://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">http://www.seask
 y.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
