BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:March Equinox
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20190320
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20190320
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a13cc518-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert4965f778bfd32c47a72a96180cc5aed8\nMarch 20 - March Eq
 uinox. The March equinox occurs at 21:58 UTC. The Sun will shine directly 
 on the equator and there will be nearly equal amounts of day and night thr
 oughout the world. This is also the first day of spring (vernal equinox) i
 n the Northern Hemisphere and the first day of fall (autumnal equinox) in 
 the Southern Hemisphere.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astrono
 my/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy
 -calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170510
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170510
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a13fb476-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertfe6c78ea34fabe4b365c928a3f4d8cce\nMay 10 - Full Moon.
  The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun and
  its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 21:42 UT
 C. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full Fl
 ower Moon because this was the time of year when spring flowers appeared i
 n abundance. This moon has also been known as the Full Corn Planting Moon 
 and the Milk Moon.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/ast
 ronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calen
 dar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kunkle Park Star Party
DTSTART:20180812T010000Z
DTEND:20180812T020000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a1428cdc-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:5v4r19teatusp9hio2k98kic89@google.com\nSee the Perseid meteor s
 hower (naked-eye\, without a telescope).  The planet Mars with its polar c
 aps will be visible throughout the evening as will Jupiter and several of 
 its large moons. \n \nSaturn with its beautiful ring system and several mo
 ons will be a showpiece and the small blue-green dot of the sun’s most d
 istant planet\, Neptune\, will rise around 10 PM.  We will see beautiful s
 tar clusters in our Milky Way Galaxy.  Planetary nebulae\, the remnants of
  exploded stars like our sun will also be visible.  Distant galaxies will 
 also be observed. \n\n\nThey are all part of the show during the Amateur A
 stronomers Association of Pittsburgh’s FREE Star Gazing Party at Kunkle 
 Park\, in Washington Township (south of Apollo).  This star gazing party i
 s scheduled for Saturday\, August 11\, 2018\, starting at dusk\, WEATHER P
 ERMITTING.  \n\nKunkle Park is located behind the Washington Township Muni
 cipal Building.  For GPS users\, that’s 285 Pine Run Church Road\, Apoll
 o\, PA 15613.  (Near the State Police barracks on Route 66.)  GPS users ca
 n also insert the following coordinates: 40 degrees\, 32.92 minutes north 
 latitude\, 79 degrees\, 36.26 minutes west longitude.\n\nFor more astronom
 y information and details about AAAP club meetings and observatories\, che
 ck out our web page at: www.3ap.org\n\nContact: Terry Trees - (724) 337-32
 31 - ( treest@comcast.net )
LOCATION:285 Pine Run Church Rd\, Apollo\, PA 15613\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170128
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170128
DTSTAMP:20260224T194010Z
UID:a14780e8-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert79801781b11afdeb7b051af83cdd1208\nJanuary 28 - New Mo
 on. The Moon will located on the same side of the Earth as the Sun and wil
 l not be visible in the night sky. This phase occurs at 00:07 UTC. This is
  the best time of the month to observe faint objects such as galaxies and 
 star clusters because there is no moonlight to interfere.\n\nSource: <a hr
 ef="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://w
 ww.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
