BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MembMtgLargeHadronCollider:
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20090410T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20090410T203000
DTSTAMP:20260224T194016Z
UID:a50a09d0-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:ckt2h7vh6fd0gajrl486qf6bd8@google.com\nApril Program:  Friday\,
  April 10\, 2009\, in the Main Auditorium of the\nCarnegie Science Center\
 , Professor Thomas\nFerguson of Carnegie Mellon University will be\ndiscus
 sing The Large Hadron Collider: Exploring a\nNew Energy Frontier.\n\nFrom 
 the abstract: “The Large Hadron Collider (LHC) is a protonproton\ncollid
 ing-beam accelerator\, situated at the European\naccelerator center\, CERN
 \, outside Geneva\, Switzerland. The\nenergy of the LHC is a factor of 7\,
  higher than the present highestenergy\naccelerator\, the Tevatron at Ferm
 ilab\, thus opening up an\nentirely new energy regime for study. The LHC w
 ill begin a yearlong\nrun starting in September\, 2009. I will give a shor
 t introduction\non the history of colliding-beam accelerators and the Stan
 dard\nModel of particle physics. I will then discuss the LHC itself\, the\
 nCompact Muon Solenoid (CMS) experiment\, of which I am a part\,\nand the 
 possible discoveries that could come from the LHC.”\n\nWho wants to be o
 n the cutting edge of partic le physics? Who\nwants to join the hunt for t
 he elusive Higgs boson\, which would\ncomplete the Standard Model of parti
 cle physics? Who hopes the\nLHC will find the missing dark matter?\n\nAs s
 ubmitted by Ann.\n\n\nKD\n\n\nAAAP monthly meetings\, featured speaker fol
 lowing business meeting\, (Detail in GS newsletter.)\, occur\, 2nd Friday 
 of the month\, 7:30 PM\, September through May\, Carnegie Science Center\,
  Pittsburgh\, Main Auditorium\, $3 Parking Fee at CSC Parking Lot. Excepti
 on:  December Meeting/Holiday Party held in an alternate location. Althoug
 h club meetings are private\, members may bring accompanying family and fr
 iends as guests. 
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20190815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20190815
DTSTAMP:20260224T194016Z
UID:a50f1394-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert51e317717b3a4e396d1fdab32db6b529\nAugust 15 - Full Mo
 on. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun 
 and its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 12:30
  UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full
  Sturgeon Moon because the large sturgeon fish of the Great Lakes and othe
 r major lakes were more easily caught at this time of year. This moon has 
 also been known as the Green Corn Moon and the Grain Moon.\n\nSource: <a h
 ref="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">http://
 www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Total Lunar Eclipse
DTSTART:20140415T045200Z
DTEND:20140415T055200Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194016Z
UID:a511e29a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:5g5tkeg3o64u95muo96k70jua4@google.com\nFor more info: http://ni
 ghtsky.jpl.nasa.gov/event-view.cfm?Event_ID=55137\n\n<a href="http://night
 sky.jpl.nasa.gov/event-view.cfm?Event_ID=55137"> TLE Mingo</a>\n\n\nFrom h
 ttp://www.almanac.com/astronomy/eclipse-dates\n<a href="http://www.almanac
 .com/astronomy/eclipse-dates">Almanac eclipse Dates</a>\n\nApril 15\, 2014
 : Total Eclipse of the Moon. This lunar eclipse is almost fully visible fr
 om North America. The Moon will enter the penumbra at 12:52 am EDT on Apri
 l 15th (9:52 pm PDT on April 14th) and will leave the penumbra at 6:39 am 
 EDT (3:39 am PDT) on April 15th.\n\nKD 10/15/2013\n\nNext TLE is Wednesday
 \, Oct 8\, 2014 and is best viewed in the Pacific Coast area\, though it w
 ill be visible here from 6:25 to 7:24 AM.  Sunrise is close to 7:24 AM tha
 t morning.  \n\nKD 4/4/2014\n\nThe Oct 8 event is in the early morning hou
 rs and we will not be able to watch all of that eclipse.  Our best bet is 
 the Sept 27/28\, 2015 Lunar Eclipse\, which reaches mid-eclipse at 10:48 P
 M EDLT on that Sunday night.  We will be listing it on next year's Star Pa
 rty Schedule at Wagman as\, I'm sure\, they will do for Mingo.  Just an ea
 rly heads up to everyone.\n \nTom \nKD 4/14/2014
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
