BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180616
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180616
DTSTAMP:20260224T194017Z
UID:a573b1a0-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConverta1b50c2a6ae9fc353ebdeebb1f28e09d\nJune 13 - New Moon.
  The Moon will located on the same side of the Earth as the Sun and will n
 ot be visible in the night sky. This phase occurs at 19:44 UTC. This is th
 e best time of the month to observe faint objects such as galaxies and sta
 r clusters because there is no moonlight to interfere.\n\nSource: <a href=
 "http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www.
 seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171005
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171005
DTSTAMP:20260224T194017Z
UID:a578935a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert5edb69652f57108cff275642f706cf64\nOctober 5 - Full Mo
 on. Moon will be directly opposite the Earth from the Sun and will be full
 y illuminated as seen from Earth. This phase occurs at 18:40 UTC. This ful
 l moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full Hunters Moon 
 because at this time of year the leaves are falling and the game is fat an
 d ready to hunt. This moon has also been known as the Travel Moon and the 
 Blood Moon. This moon is also known as the Harvest Moon. The Harvest Moon 
 is the full moon that occurs closest to the September equinox each year.\n
 \nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017
 .html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
