BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Meteors! Comet 209P/LINEAR
DTSTART:20140524T060000Z
DTEND:20140524T070000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194019Z
UID:a6b89da0-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:tupoa6cho4br90nl5lut7ah8ak@google.com\nMeteors Pittsburgh Meteo
 rs\n\nMingo Observatory will be open to members and the public to observe 
 the potential meteor shower starting at 11:55 PM on Friday and remain open
  all night if it is clear. Call the observatory at 724-348-6150  if there 
 are any doubts as to weather conditions.\n\nMore info:  http://nightsky.jp
 l.nasa.gov/event-view.cfm?Event_ID=57451\n\n<a href=" http://nightsky.jpl.
 nasa.gov/event-view.cfm?Event_ID=57451">Mingo Camelopardalid Meteor Viewin
 g</a> \n\nKen K.\n\nKD 5/21/2014\n-----------------------\nMay Cameloparda
 lids - Comet 209P/LINEAR .  \n\nMingo Observatory\n\nAlthough there are no
  definite plans to open AAAP's observatories at this time\, there may be o
 rganized viewing arranged depending upon the sky conditions and staffing a
 vailability. Currently Pittsburgh weather for late May looks cloudy/rainy 
 and may not allow meteor viewing from our location.\n\nMaybe a meteor show
 er of note.\n\nTelescopes are not needed to view meteors\, only your eyes 
 and clear dark skies.  Suggest 2-4 AM May 24 for the \n\n\nhttp://science.
 nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2014/06may_newshower/ (May6)\n\n"Com
 et 209P/LINEAR's next perihelion occurs on May 6\, 2014\, at a point 0.969
  astronomical unit from the Sun and with Earth not far away. "\n\n"The bes
 t part of all this prognostication\, at least from a U.S. perspective\, is
  that the meteor display should peak between 7:00 and 8:00 Universal Time 
 on May 24th — it'll be dark across virtually all of North America."\n\n"
 And the Moon will be a narrow waning sliver just a few days from new. Ever
 ything is "go" for the best meteor display since the dramatic Leonid showe
 rs of the late 1990s."\n\nhttp://www.skyandtelescope.com/observing/home/17
 3316061.html\n\n\n"The tried-and-true list of showers might change soon\, 
 though. As we noted in 2012\, the comet 209P/LINEAR\, which passed by the 
 Sun in 2009\, may produce a big meteor shower on May 24\, 2014. New calcul
 ations by Quanzhi Ye and Paul A. Wiegert (both University of Western Ontar
 io) refine that prediction — and make it a little less optimistic. " htt
 p://www.skyandtelescope.com/observing/highlights/The-Next-New-Meteor-Showe
 r-231466791.html\n\nAlso:  http://arxiv.org/abs/1311.0235\n\nKD 12/22/2013
   updated May 6
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jupiter at Opposition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20190610
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20190610
DTSTAMP:20260224T194019Z
UID:a6c5ca7a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertace4bff6550c416896d15e78c8219d0b\nJune 10 - Jupiter a
 t Opposition. The giant planet will be at its closest approach to Earth an
 d its face will be fully illuminated by the Sun. It will be brighter than 
 any other time of the year and will be visible all night long. This is the
  best time to view and photograph Jupiter and its moons. A medium-sized te
 lescope should be able to show you some of the details in Jupiter's cloud 
 bands. A good pair of binoculars should allow you to see Jupiter's four la
 rgest moons appearing as bright dots on either side of the planet.\n\nSour
 ce: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html"
 >http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
