UID:a875b074-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:20080312T190940Z-2311450-32526@www.3ap.org\nOkay.  Here are the
  Metzgar dates.  All are on Fridays\, except for the lunar eclipse on Wedn
 esday Feb. 20.\n\n \n\nFeb 8\n\nFeb 20\n\nMar 7\n\nApr 11 \n\nMay 9\n\n \n
 \nThanks\,\n\nJim\n\nPrivate S.P. \, New Alex.\n\nPrivate Star Party \, Ne
 w Alexandria\, Near Greensburg.\nMetzgar school astronomy club will have t
 wo star parties next month(Feb) - one on the 8th\, a moonless night to lea
 rn the winter constellations\, and take in a few star\nclusters\, and then
  again on the 20th\, for the lunar eclipse. \n\nLater in the\nspring when 
 it gets warmer\, I'm hoping I can entice a few AAAP\nmembers to join me\, 
 to give the kids an opportunity to look through a greater\nvariety of tele
 scopes. \n\nI'm also hoping to take them to Wagman for a "fieldtrip"\, ...
 .... If anyone from the club lives in the Greensburg area and would be wil
 ling to come to one of our club star parties in the future\, please let me
  know. \n\nThe Metzgar Astronomy Club will have two additional private sta
 r parties later this spring.  The first will be on March 7th and the secon
 d on May 9th. ..........  I hope to take the kids to Wagman on April 11th.
   Our astronomy club meets in the parking lot of Metzgar Elementary School
  around dusk. \n\nFor our May meeting we will probably meet around 7:00 or
  7:30 and launch some model rockets prior to doing our observing when it g
 ets dark.  I would ask that anyone interested in attending to please conta
 ct me in advance.\n\n............. I have permission from the school to ho
 st this event for the kids and their families\, but I havent asked them 
 if it would be okay for me to open this up to just anyone at large. (These
  are Private Events posted on the web calendar for AAAP Members\, to plan 
 their participation.)\n\nClear skies to all\,\nJim \nAAAP (NSN) Member\, C
 o-Sponsor Metzgar Astronomy Club \nNew Alexandria\, PA\nSee AAAP List of J
 an 18\, for Jim's email and more info. \n\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jupiter at Opposition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180509
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180509
DTSTAMP:20260224T194022Z
UID:a87d108a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert5d14d3382344b3390a46da75477f73b6\nMay 9 - Jupiter at 
 Opposition. The giant planet will be at its closest approach to Earth and 
 its face will be fully illuminated by the Sun. It will be brighter than an
 y other time of the year and will be visible all night long. This is the b
 est time to view and photograph Jupiter and its moons. A medium-sized tele
 scope should be able to show you some of the details in Jupiter's cloud ba
 nds. A good pair of binoculars should allow you to see Jupiter's four larg
 est moons appearing as bright dots on either side of the planet.\n\nSource
 : <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">h
 ttp://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170609
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170609
DTSTAMP:20260224T194022Z
UID:a882b396-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert6484e7fd1f714ae2cafcc3bfbbd2196d\nJune 9 - Full Moon.
  The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun and
  its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 13:10 UT
 C. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full St
 rawberry Moon because it signaled the time of year to gather ripening frui
 t. It also coincides with the peak of the strawberry harvesting season. Th
 is moon has also been known as the Full Rose Moon and the Full Honey Moon.
 \n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-20
 17.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
