BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Lyrid Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20140422
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20140422
DTSTAMP:20260224T194023Z
UID:a96025d2-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:u1ovk6t1840oqprfmuuctjtlr8@google.com\nApril 22\, 23 - Lyrids M
 eteor Shower. The Lyrids is an average shower\, usually producing about 20
  meteors per hour at its peak. It is produced by dust particles left behin
 d by comet C/1861 G1 Thatcher\, which was discovered in 1861. The shower r
 uns annually from April 16-25. It peaks this year on the night of the nigh
 t of the 22nd and morning of the 23rd. These meteors can sometimes produce
  bright dust trails that last for several seconds. The second quarter moon
  will be a slight problem this year\, blocking the less bright meteors fro
 m view. Best viewing will be from a dark location after midnight. Meteors 
 will radiate from the constellation Lyra\, but can appear anywhere in the 
 sky.\n\nhttp://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2014.html\n\nKD
  12/22/2013
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mars at Opposition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20160522
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20160522
DTSTAMP:20260224T194023Z
UID:a9636404-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:qb9hok04nm4b92j3rbr73lehgc@google.com\nAlthough Mars is our nex
 t door neighbor\, it’s the second smallest planet in the Solar System\, 
 making it difficult to observe in detail through a telescope. Your best sh
 ot will come on May 22\, when Mars and Earth reach their closest points to
  one another. Try viewing Mars in a large telescope to see if you can spot
  its polar ice caps or any of the darker regions on the rusty-red surface.
   \n\nSource: <a href="http://www.telescopes.com/blogs/helpful-information
 /46104069-the-best-celestial-events-of-2016">http://www.telescopes.com/blo
 gs/helpful-information/46104069-the-best-celestial-events-of-2016</a>\n\nP
 osted by Kathy Desantis ( <a href="mailto:kappa@3ap.org">kappa@3ap.org</a>
  ).
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zoo-Wild Earth Day
DTSTART:20140425T140000Z
DTEND:20140425T150000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194023Z
UID:a9693406-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:92naamj94rtvsqp105oitp1tjs@google.com\nConservation Education D
 epartment\nWILD EARTH DAY  10AM-1:30PM Friday\, April 25\nat the Pittsburg
 h Zoo & PPG Aquarium\n\n\n\nSaturday at the Zoo April 26\, 2014 11AM-3PM\n
 \nParty for the Planet Saturday\, April 26\, 2014 11AM-3PM\n\nExhibitor se
 t-up will begin at 9AM. All exhibitors must be unloaded and set-up by 10:3
 0AM. Table-covers and skirting are not provided by the Zoo. Items such as 
 balloons\, straws\, and Frisbees are not permitted in the Zoo for the safe
 ty of our animals. If you plan on distributing a “promotional giveaway
 ” to Zoo visitors\, please have this item cleared with the Zoo ahead of 
 time. \n\n \n \nHere are two events  at the Pittsburgh Zoo http://www.pitt
 sburghzoo.org/ where AAAP has an opportunity to share the schedules for th
 e 2014 Star Party Season and astronomy outreach.  More than one person can
  volunteer.\n \nIf you are new to the club. Understand at opportunities li
 ke this members like to do one or more of these:\n \n1. Share star party s
 chedules.\n2. Offer telescope viewing - Moon\, bright planets\, distant ob
 jects/buildings.\n3. Solar viewing.\n4. Hands on activities like assemblin
 g "star wheels\," folding cash register tape to demonstrate distance in th
 e solar system\, manipulating "foam and sticks" to show refraction\, marbl
 e and golf tee to show "plant wobble" and albedo changes used to discover 
 new planets\, etc. Hundreds of activity ideas are available through the Ni
 ght Sky Network http://nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov/download-search.cfm\n5. GLOBE
  at Night http://www.globeatnight.org/\n6. Share their own astrophotograph
 s \n7. Talk about their telescope\, telescopes in general\, astronomy\, et
 c
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180826
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180826
DTSTAMP:20260224T194023Z
UID:a96edc4e-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert6dca9a6149133b4dc6a1f04717d095b7\nAugust 26 - Full Mo
 on. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun 
 and its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 11:57
  UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full
  Sturgeon Moon because the large sturgeon fish of the Great Lakes and othe
 r major lakes were more easily caught at this time of year. This moon has 
 also been known as the Green Corn Moon and the Grain Moon.\n\nSource: <a h
 ref="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://
 www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Uranus at Opposition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20191027
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20191027
DTSTAMP:20260224T194023Z
UID:a971c49a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvertb16c4cb8e10d8d8600e02e4de787b030\nOctober 27 - Uranus
  at Opposition. The blue-green planet will be at its closest approach to E
 arth and its face will be fully illuminated by the Sun. It will be brighte
 r than any other time of the year and will be visible all night long. This
  is the best time to view Uranus. Due to its distance it will only appear 
 as a tiny blue-green dot in all but the most powerful telescopes.\n\nSourc
 e: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">
 http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
