BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CANCELLED N. Allegheny/McKnight Elementary's Science Night 
DTSTART:20160401T220000Z
DTEND:20160401T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194024Z
UID:a9b801f8-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:5a3ndmvc5esq5t2qn01amngdeo@google.com\nCancelled due to clouds.
  KD 4/1/2016 as per Fred K on AAAPgh\n\nhttp://www.northallegheny.org/page
 /4045\n\nDennis Morton and the Amateur Astronomers Associationof Pittsburg
 h (3ap.org) invite all North Allegheny students and their families to shar
 e the night sky.\n\nAAAPers have been showing up for the North Allegheny S
 cience night setting up scopes in front of the school.   AAAP members are 
 welcome to join in.  They always have a large crowd and it is always a cle
 ar night.  Please let me know if you would like to help out. Please contac
 t mcksciencenight@gmail.com if you plan to attend.\n\nMcKnight Elementary 
 School of North Allegheny schools\, 500 Cumberland Rd\, Pittsburgh\, PA 15
 237.\n\n"We will setup in the traffic semi-circle just west of the school.
   The event is 6-8 PM\, but the star party goes on for another half-hour o
 r so.  For most of that time\, we only get a few people who are going in t
 o the science event in the school\, but we get many when leaving and the l
 ines get long for a little while.  So arriving later would be OK. Note tha
 t the sun doesn’t set until 7:45 that night\, so it will be light.  Sola
 r scopes would be welcome.  Jupiter rises at 5:18.  Mercury is 9 degrees a
 bove the sun.  No moon or other bright planets will be up that night."  as
  per Fred\n\n\n
LOCATION:McKnight Middle School \, 500 Cumberland Rd\, Pittsburgh\, PA 1523
 7
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Geminids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171213
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171213
DTSTAMP:20260224T194024Z
UID:a9bd1e72-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert525eba4701cdc27270b6d149c98b1f99\nDecember 13-14 - Ge
 minids Meteor Shower. The Geminids is the king of the meteor showers. It i
 s considered by many to be the best shower in the heavens producing up to 
 120 multicolored meteors per hour at its peak. It is produced by debris le
 ft behind by an asteroid known as 3200 Phaethon which was discovered in 19
 82. The shower runs annually from December 7-17. It peaks this year on the
  night of the 13th and morning of the 14th. The waning crescent moon will 
 be no match for the Geminids this year. The skies should still be dark eno
 ugh for an excellent show. Best viewing will be from a dark location after
  midnight. Meteors will radiate from the constellation Gemini but can appe
 ar anywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronom
 y/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-
 calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
