BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180529
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180529
DTSTAMP:20260224T194029Z
UID:ac91dee4-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert00cebb0e3ca02cc0f58f59078e228786\nMay 29 - Full Moon.
  The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun and
  its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 14:19 UT
 C. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full Fl
 ower Moon because this was the time of year when spring flowers appeared i
 n abundance. This moon has also been known as the Full Corn Planting Moon 
 and the Milk Moon.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/ast
 ronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calen
 dar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20170226
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20170226
DTSTAMP:20260224T194029Z
UID:ac956dc0-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert2fc93e1a5745b43cc6567e438ab6b546\nFebruary 26 - New M
 oon. The Moon will located on the same side of the Earth as the Sun and wi
 ll not be visible in the night sky. This phase occurs at 14:59 UTC. This i
 s the best time of the month to observe faint objects such as galaxies and
  star clusters because there is no moonlight to interfere.\n\nSource: <a h
 ref="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http://
 www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon & Supermoon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20190219
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20190219
DTSTAMP:20260224T194029Z
UID:ac9afd8a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert7ef0c79668f8f7034b4fcdcdb4d3e219\nFebruary 19 - Full 
 Moon & Supermoon. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Ear
 th as the Sun and its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase o
 ccurs at 15:53 UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American trib
 es as the Full Snow Moon because the heaviest snows usually fell during th
 is time of the year. Since hunting is difficult this moon has also been kn
 own by some tribes as the Full Hunger Moon since the harsh weather made hu
 nting difficult. This is also the second of three supermoons for 2019. The
  Moon will be at its closest approach to the Earth and may look slightly l
 arger and brighter than usual.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/a
 stronomy/astronomy-calendar-2019.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/ast
 ronomy-calendar-2019.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mercury at Greatest Eastern Elongation
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171124
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171124
DTSTAMP:20260224T194029Z
UID:ac9e7348-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert9311b6bdbe3caa3ba1d6b3cf7c93d4db\nNovember 24 - Mercu
 ry at Greatest Eastern Elongation. The planet Mercury reaches greatest eas
 tern elongation of 22.0 degrees from the Sun. This is the best time to vie
 w Mercury since it will be at its highest point above the horizon in the e
 vening sky. Look for the planet low in the western sky just after sunset.\
 n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-201
 7.html">http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
