BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Astronomy Lecture
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20140923T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20140923T203000
DTSTAMP:20260224T194030Z
UID:ad2a11be-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:vtmikrdfbi0r7kngu7184nm438_R20140916T233000@google.com\nSession
  3 on Sept. 23 is about when the universe rapidly expanded and gravity "de
 coupled" from other forces\, which include the strong nuclear force\, the 
 weak nuclear force and electromagnetism.\n\nLecture: The Big Bang and Then
  Some – a Layman’s Perspective\n\nLearn about how everything we now en
 joy in the night sky got started.\n\nhttps://nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov/club/ev
 ent-view.cfm?Event_ID=59333\n \nOpen Rain or shine\, cloudy or clear\n\nFo
 ur Tuesdays\, 7:30 PM – 9:00 PM\,  September 9th\, 16th\, 23rd\, and 30t
 h\n\n<a href="http://www.post-gazette.com/local/south/2014/08/28/Seminar-s
 eries-takes-a-look-at-theBig-Bang/stories/201408250220#ixzz3CkN7WGSj">PG a
 rticle</a>\n\nKD 7/16/2014
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pgh Constellation Closing Reception
DTSTART:20160326T230000Z
DTEND:20160327T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194030Z
UID:ad30006a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:bs27ble596pnflhspv14deafj8@google.com\nAssemble's website descr
 ibing the month:\nhttp://assemblepgh.org/event/our-pittsburgh-constellatio
 n/\n\n"The closing reception for the "Our Pittsburgh Constellation" month-
 long extravaganza of astronomy and art will be held on Friday\, March 25\,
  2016\, from 6 to 9 pm. Join us for a display of telescopes while members 
 of the Amateur Astronomers Association of Pittsburgh (AAAP\, 3ap.org) shar
 e their knowledge and teach how to make star wheels to navigate the night 
 sky. Have you ever wondered how to find the best telescope in your price r
 ange? What accessories make observing easier? How does one take those gorg
 eous astrophotographs? Take advantage of this free\, public\, rain-or-shin
 e\, indoor activity. No registration required. "\nFrom DT AAAPgh Post of 2
 /26/2016\n\nThe City of Pittsburgh’s Office of Sustainability invites yo
 u to partake of a month of activities centered on astronomy and light poll
 ution solutions. “Our Pittsburgh Constellation” includes a space art g
 allery show\, talks\, workshops and demonstrations. It begins with an open
 ing reception on Friday\, March 4\, 2016 – come hear the City’s Chief 
 Innovation and Performance Officer Debra Lam open the show at 7:00 pm\, th
 en stay and look at celestial art and talk with a dozen local artists.  In
 formation on the public workshops connecting visual art and space can be f
 ound on the Assemble calendar and on pghconstellation.com\, the website li
 sting events of possible interest to astronomy enthusiasts in the Pittsbur
 gh area. The exhibit’s closing reception will be held on Saturday\, Marc
 h 26\, 2016. Some workshops are free\; some have fees and may require regi
 stration. A sample of the month’s activities:\n\nBead necklaces with mot
 her-of-pearl phases of the Moon\nMake needle felted planets\nLearn about o
 rbital trajectories with light photography\nListen to the plan for LED str
 eetlights for a smart city\nTalk to astronomers and artists\nPaint nebulae
  and other celestial objects\nTalk to science fiction authors about their 
 envisioned future of space\nCreate astro-journals\nActivities are subsidiz
 ed by the City of Pittsburgh through the Heinz Endowments grant\, “Our A
 stronomical Footprint\,” using art to make a positive change in the worl
 d. We hope to draw attention to International Earth Hour celebrations on M
 arch 19\, 2016 from 8:30 to 9:30 pm – turn off your outdoor lights to sh
 ow that we are stewards of the planet. Join us in our enjoyment of the hea
 vens and our desire to reduce light pollution.\n\n\nIf you click on the in
 dividual kid's links on the calendar\, it brings up the times and age grou
 ps involved:\nhttp://assemblepgh.org/events/2016-03/\n\nAssemble\, a commu
 nity space for arts and tech\n5125 Penn Ave\nPittsburgh PA 15224\n(412) 43
 2-9127\ninfo@assemblepgh.org\n\n\nContact Diane T.\n\nKD 2/12/2015\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Venus at Greatest Eastern Elongation
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180817
DTSTAMP:20260224T194030Z
UID:ad39d9aa-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert6c856ce28d01d268fad03106ef2babc7\nAugust 17 - Venus a
 t Greatest Eastern Elongation. The planet Venus reaches greatest eastern e
 longation of 45.9 degrees from the Sun. This is the best time to view Venu
 s since it will be at its highest point above the horizon in the evening s
 ky. Look for the bright planet in the western sky after sunset.\n\nSource:
  <a href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">ht
 tp://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ursids Meteor Shower
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20171221
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20171221
DTSTAMP:20260224T194030Z
UID:ad4058f2-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert83bd401baa2b6e306a35814f507b8d15\nDecember 21-22 - Ur
 sids Meteor Shower. The Ursids is a minor meteor shower producing about 5-
 10 meteors per hour. It is produced by dust grains left behind by comet Tu
 ttle which was first discovered in 1790. The shower runs annually from Dec
 ember 17-25. It peaks this year on the the night of the 21st and morning o
 f the 22nd. The crescent moon will set early in the evening leaving dark s
 kies for optimal observing. Best viewing will be just after midnight from 
 a dark location far away from city lights. Meteors will radiate from the c
 onstellation Ursa Minor but can appear anywhere in the sky.\n\nSource: <a 
 href="http://www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html">http:/
 /www.seasky.org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2017.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
