BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Full Moon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180430
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20180430
DTSTAMP:20260224T194033Z
UID:af03d42a-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:CSVConvert56dcfb151e365988efce3f9278c7d3a2\nApril 30 - Full Moo
 n. The Moon will be located on the opposite side of the Earth as the Sun a
 nd its face will be will be fully illuminated. This phase occurs at 00:58 
 UTC. This full moon was known by early Native American tribes as the Full 
 Pink Moon because it marked the appearance of the moss pink or wild ground
  phlox which is one of the first spring flowers. This moon has also been k
 nown as the Sprouting Grass Moon\; the Growing Moon\; and the Egg Moon. Ma
 ny coastal tribes called it the Full Fish Moon because this was the time t
 hat the shad swam upstream to spawn.\n\nSource: <a href="http://www.seasky
 .org/astronomy/astronomy-calendar-2018.html">http://www.seasky.org/astrono
 my/astronomy-calendar-2018.html</a>
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pgh Constellation Opening Reception
DTSTART:20160305T000000Z
DTEND:20160305T010000Z
DTSTAMP:20260224T194033Z
UID:af09b282-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:e5265htsc40klip5g8ggumnlk8@google.com\nAssemble's website descr
 ibing the month:\nhttp://assemblepgh.org/event/our-pittsburgh-constellatio
 n/\n\nThe City of Pittsburgh’s Office of Sustainability invites you to p
 artake of a month of activities centered on astronomy and light pollution 
 solutions. “Our Pittsburgh Constellation” includes a space art gallery
  show\, talks\, workshops and demonstrations. It begins with an opening re
 ception on Friday\, March 4\, 2016 – come hear the City’s Chief Innova
 tion and Performance Officer Debra Lam open the show at 7:00 pm\, then sta
 y and look at celestial art and talk with a dozen local artists.  Informat
 ion on the public workshops connecting visual art and space can be found o
 n the Assemble calendar and on pghconstellation.com\, the website listing 
 events of possible interest to astronomy enthusiasts in the Pittsburgh are
 a. The exhibit’s closing reception will be held on Saturday\, March 26\,
  2016. Some workshops are free\; some have fees and may require registrati
 on. A sample of the month’s activities:\n\nBead necklaces with mother-of
 -pearl phases of the Moon\nMake needle felted planets\nLearn about orbital
  trajectories with light photography\nListen to the plan for LED streetlig
 hts for a smart city\nTalk to astronomers and artists\nPaint nebulae and o
 ther celestial objects\nTalk to science fiction authors about their envisi
 oned future of space\nCreate astro-journals\nActivities are subsidized by 
 the City of Pittsburgh through the Heinz Endowments grant\, “Our Astrono
 mical Footprint\,” using art to make a positive change in the world. We 
 hope to draw attention to International Earth Hour celebrations on March 1
 9\, 2016 from 8:30 to 9:30 pm – turn off your outdoor lights to show tha
 t we are stewards of the planet. Join us in our enjoyment of the heavens a
 nd our desire to reduce light pollution.\n\n\nIf you click on the individu
 al kid's links on the calendar\, it brings up the times and age groups inv
 olved:\nhttp://assemblepgh.org/events/2016-03/\n\nAssemble\, a community s
 pace for arts and tech\n5125 Penn Ave\nPittsburgh PA 15224\n(412) 432-9127
 \ninfo@assemblepgh.org\n\n\nContact Diane T.\n\nKD 2/12/2015\n
LOCATION:None
END:VEVENT
