BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Hartley-id Watch
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20101102
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20101102
DTSTAMP:20260224T194043Z
UID:b560b392-11b8-11f1-ab09-766b39882e0f
DESCRIPTION:i5m534q0osh2hs236n8r9sgj5o@google.com\n<p>Hartley-id Watch</p>\
 n<p> <a href="http://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2010/27
 oct_hartleyids/"\n   \n > http://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-
 nasa/2010/27oct_hartleyids/</a></p>\n\n<p>\n"If there is a <b>Hartley-id s
 hower—"that's a big IF</b>\," notes Cooke--it would emanate from the con
 stellation <b>Cygnus the Swan\,</b> visible to observers in the northern h
 emisphere almost <b>directly overhead after sunset in early November</b>. 
 Lunar interference should not be a problem. On <b>Nov. 2nd and 3rd</b>\, t
 he Moon will be a slender crescent\, providing dark skies for a meteor wat
 ch. "\n\n</p>\n\n<p><a href ="http://earthsky.org/tonight/summer-triangle-
 deneb-and-its-constellation-cygnus-2">Cygnus</a>\n</p>\n\nKD
LOCATION:Cygnus the Swan\, Sky Overhead
END:VEVENT
